.Dd May 23, 2026
.Dt NEIGHBOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm neighbot
.Nd network neighbor monitoring daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl B Ar seconds
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl e Ar days
.Op Fl f Ar dbfile
.Op Fl i Ar iface
.Op Fl m Ar mailto
.Op Fl o Ar ouifile
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl s Ar sendmail
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
passively monitors ARP (IPv4) and NDP (IPv6) traffic on all Ethernet
interfaces.
It maintains a database of IP-to-MAC address mappings and sends email
alerts when new stations appear or existing mappings change.
Notifications include reverse DNS hostname, hardware vendor
(from the OUI database, if available), and timestamps.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl B Ar seconds
Bogon cooldown.
Suppress duplicate bogon log lines and email notifications for the
same IP and interface within
.Ar seconds .
Bogons are not stored in the database, so this cooldown is what bounds
per-address reporting.
Default: 1800 (30 minutes).
Set to 0 to disable this per-address cooldown; a global rate cap on
total bogon reporting still applies as a flood backstop.
.It Fl d
Daemonize.
Detach from the terminal and log to syslog instead of stderr.
.It Fl e Ar days
Drop entries that have not been seen for more than
.Ar days
days.
The check runs once per hour.
Set to 0 (default) to disable this expiry; non-EUI-64, non-link-local
IPv6 entries are still dropped after 7 days unseen (see
.Sx OBSOLETE ADDRESS CLEANUP ) .
This is independent of probe-driven cleanup, which always removes
entries whose IP no longer responds at the recorded MAC.
.It Fl f Ar dbfile
Path to the database file.
If the file exists but cannot be read in full,
.Nm
exits instead of overwriting it.
Default:
.Pa /var/neighbot/neighbot.csv .
.It Fl i Ar iface
Monitor only the specified interface instead of all Ethernet interfaces.
When monitoring all interfaces (no
.Fl i
specified), trunk interfaces that have VLAN subinterfaces and no
addresses of their own other than link-local ones are automatically
skipped.
Only the VLAN subinterfaces are monitored.
Trunks with other addresses are kept since they carry native VLAN
traffic.
On Linux, this requires the 8021q kernel module to be loaded.
On Linux, bridge ports and bonding slaves are skipped as well, since
their traffic is seen on the bridge or bond device; a bridge device is
captured in promiscuous mode so that unicast frames forwarded between
its ports, such as a spoofed ARP reply to one host, are seen too.
Use
.Fl i
to explicitly monitor a trunk interface, bridge port or bond slave.
Interfaces whose name contains a comma or a control character are
never monitored, since the name is stored unquoted in the CSV database.
.It Fl m Ar mailto
Email recipient for notifications.
Default:
.Ql root .
.It Fl o Ar ouifile
Path to the OUI vendor database file.
Both the neighbot format
.Pq aa:bb:cc Vendor
and the arp-scan format
.Pq AABBCC<tab>Vendor
are supported.
Default:
.Pa /var/neighbot/oui.txt .
.It Fl p
Disable active probing.
By default, when a known MAC address appears at a new IP,
.Nm
sends ARP requests or NDP Neighbor Solicitations to each old IP
of the same address family to determine whether the device moved
(old IP dead) or has multiple addresses (both active).
Dual-stack hosts are not probed across IPv4/IPv6.
Probes are sent from the interface's own address, the link-local one
for IPv6, since a probe from the zero address looks like duplicate
address detection to a host that is bringing up the probed address.
An interface with no IPv4 address other than a link-local one, or no
IPv6 link-local address, sends from the zero address.
Link-local addresses and IPs assigned to the local host's own
interfaces are excluded from probing.
This flag makes
.Nm
purely passive.
.Pp
When an IPv6 temporary address (RFC 4941) rotates, the new address
is not reported as a new station; older temporary addresses are not
probed, and are reaped later by the stale non-EUI-64 sweep (see
.Sx OBSOLETE ADDRESS CLEANUP ) .
This also covers link-local address rotation on devices with
randomized MACs, although link-local entries are only removed by
.Fl e .
This handling does not depend on probing and also applies with
.Fl p .
.It Fl q
Quiet mode.
Suppress email notifications.
Events are still logged.
.It Fl r
Report mode.
Load the database and print a summary of all known neighbors to
stdout, grouped by interface and MAC address.
No packet capture, privilege drop, or daemon setup is performed.
.Pp
When combined with
.Fl m ,
the report is emailed via sendmail instead of printed.
When combined with
.Fl q ,
the report is always printed to stdout.
.Pp
The OUI vendor database is the file given by
.Fl o ;
without
.Fl o ,
it is
.Pa /var/neighbot/oui.txt
if readable, else
.Pa /usr/local/share/arp-scan/ieee-oui.txt .
If it cannot be loaded, vendor names show as
.Ql <unknown> .
.It Fl s Ar sendmail
Path to the sendmail-compatible mail program used for notifications.
Default:
.Pa /usr/sbin/sendmail .
.It Fl u Ar user
Drop privileges to
.Ar user
after opening packet capture handles.
The default database directory
.Pa /var/neighbot
is chowned to this user and set to mode 0750;
a directory given with
.Fl f
is left untouched and must be writable by
.Ar user .
Default:
.Ql nobody .
.It Fl V
Print the version number and exit.
.El
.Sh EVENTS
.Nm
generates the following event types:
.Bl -tag -width "flip-flop"
.It new
A previously unknown IP address is seen for the first time.
The email notification is suppressed when an IPv6 temporary address
rotates (same MAC, same /64 prefix on the same interface, non-EUI-64).
.It changed
An IP address is seen with a different MAC address than the one
previously recorded.
.It flip-flop
An IP address alternates between two previously seen MAC addresses.
This typically indicates VRRP/HSRP failover, dual-homed hosts,
or ARP spoofing.
If the same IP changes MAC address 5 or more times within 60 seconds,
whether as flip-flop or changed events (three or more devices taking
turns), an address conflict storm is declared: a single warning email
is sent and further changed and flip-flop notifications for that IP
are suppressed until the storm
subsides (no flips for 30 minutes) or a
.Dv SIGHUP
is received.
.It reappeared
A known MAC/IP pair is seen again after 180 days or more of silence.
.It bogon
An IP address is seen that does not belong to any subnet configured
on any monitored interface.
This may indicate ARP or NDP spoofing.
Bogon packets are not added to the database.
An address already recorded on the receiving interface with the same
MAC is not reported, so stations in an RA-learned prefix stay known
after a restart until the router advertises the prefix again.
IPs that are foreign to the receiving interface but match another
monitored interface's subnet are silently ignored (common with
access points on VLAN trunks).
The configured subnets are re-read every 60 seconds, on
.Dv SIGHUP ,
and before an address is reported as a bogon (at most once a second),
so an address just added to an interface does not make its
neighbours look foreign.
.It ra-learned
A Router Advertisement announced a new on-link IPv6 prefix on an
interface.
The prefix is added to the per-interface list of known subnets so
that addresses within it are no longer reported as bogons.
An email is sent when a prefix is learned on an interface, unless it
lies inside a subnet configured on that interface (it is then only
logged); refreshes of a live prefix are silent, but a prefix that expired, was withdrawn
or was evicted from the table is reported again when re-announced.
Prefixes expire after the RA valid-lifetime (capped at 7 days), and a
prefix re-advertised with the L flag and a zero valid-lifetime is
withdrawn at once.
A prefix option without the L flag is ignored, since it says nothing
about which addresses are on-link.
Prefixes shorter than /48 are ignored and logged: real on-link
prefixes are /64, and a short prefix would whitelist an implausibly
wide slice of the address space.
.Pp
Router Advertisements are validated per RFC 4861: only RAs with an
IPv6 hop limit of 255 and a link-local source address are accepted,
so off-link or spoofed advertisements are dropped.
A rogue RA sent by an on-link attacker can still suppress bogon alerts
for the prefix it announces.
The first-learn notification is intended to surface that event so the
operator can investigate.
.It moved
A MAC address is seen at a new IP address while its old IP no longer
responds to probes, or is answered by a different MAC (requires active
probing).
The obsolete IP entry is removed from the database after the
notification is sent, unless another MAC has taken the old IP over, in
which case that is also reported as a changed station.
If the probed interface has no link (or is gone) when the probes time
out, they could not have been answered, and the probe is cancelled
instead.
.El
.Sh OBSOLETE ADDRESS CLEANUP
.Nm
removes database entries it knows are no longer valid:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Em probe-confirmed
When a probe times out, the obsolete entry is dropped (see the
.Em moved
event).
.It Em per-MAC cap
At most 256 addresses per MAC are tracked; further new addresses for
that MAC are ignored (logged once per run).
An address that moves to a MAC already at the limit is still reported
as changed, but its entry keeps the previous MAC, and rows beyond the
limit in the database file are skipped at load.
This bounds a flood of addresses under a single MAC, keeping every
per-MAC operation constant-time; a legitimate neighbour stays well
below the limit.
.It Em stale non-EUI-64 IPv6
Non-EUI-64, non-link-local IPv6 entries unseen for more than 7 days
are dropped on the hourly sweep.
Always on, regardless of
.Fl e .
The test is the address shape alone, so RFC 7217 stable-privacy,
DHCPv6, and manually assigned global addresses are dropped too: they
cannot be distinguished from RFC 4941 privacy addresses by inspection.
A host keeping such an address but silent for more than 7 days is
reported as a new station when it reappears.
EUI-64, link-local, and IPv4 addresses are not affected.
.It Em idle expiration
With
.Fl e Ar days ,
entries unseen for more than
.Ar days
days are dropped on the next hourly sweep.
Disabled by default.
.El
.Sh DATABASE FORMAT
The database is a CSV file with the following fields:
.Pp
.D1 ip,mac,interface,first_seen,last_seen,prev_mac
.Pp
The
.Ar prev_mac
field stores the previous MAC address for flip-flop detection.
Old database files without this field are loaded without errors.
.Pp
Each entry is identified by the combination of interface and address, so
the same address seen on two monitored interfaces is tracked as two
separate entries rather than reported as a conflict.
.Pp
Timestamps are in ISO 8601 format (local time).
The database is saved atomically via a temporary file and rename.
.Pp
The maximum number of entries is limited to 100000 to prevent
memory exhaustion from spoofed traffic.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGHUP
Save the database to disk (no sooner than 10 seconds after the previous
save), clear any active storm suppression and re-read the interface
addresses.
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Save the database and exit.
.It Dv SIGUSR1
Dump active probe state to the log.
.It Dv SIGPIPE
Ignored.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/neighbot/neighbot.csv"
.It Pa /var/neighbot/neighbot.csv
Default database file.
.It Pa /var/neighbot/oui.txt
OUI vendor database, installed automatically by
.Sq make install .
If present, email notifications include the hardware vendor name
resolved from the MAC address prefix.
The file is loaded once at startup.
Use
.Sq make oui-update
to re-download it into the source tree and
.Sq make install
to install it, or add a daily cron job to keep it current.
.It Pa /etc/systemd/system/neighbot.service
Systemd unit file installed by
.Sq make install-systemd
(Linux).
The .deb and .rpm packages install it under
.Pa /usr/lib/systemd/system
or
.Pa /lib/systemd/system
instead.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/neighbot
rc.d script
.Pq Ox .
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
drops to an unprivileged user
.Pq default: nobody
after opening packet capture handles.
All supplementary groups are dropped.
.Pp
On
.Ox ,
.Nm
additionally uses
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
to restrict its privileges after initialization.
.Pp
In quiet mode
.Pq Fl q ,
.Nm
pledges
.Cm stdio rpath wpath cpath route
and unveils only the database directory.
.Pp
When email notifications are enabled,
.Nm
pledges
.Cm stdio rpath wpath cpath route proc exec dns
and does not use
.Xr unveil 2
so that
.Xr sendmail 8
can access its configuration files.
.Pp
In report mode
.Pq Fl r ,
.Nm
pledges
.Cm stdio
after loading the database and OUI file.
When combined with
.Fl m ,
the pledge is
.Cm stdio proc exec
to allow sendmail execution.
.Pp
Each notification is delivered by a short-lived child process that
performs a reverse DNS lookup and execs
.Xr sendmail 8 .
To keep a flood of events, such as spoofed in-subnet ARP, from
spawning children at packet rate, no more than 32 notification
children run at once; further notifications are suppressed and
logged until the backlog clears.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on clean shutdown or after printing a report.
It exits 1 on an invalid option or argument, an unknown
.Ar user ,
a database file that exists but cannot be read, when no usable
interface is found, when it cannot detach with
.Fl d ,
when privileges cannot be dropped or
.Xr pledge 2
fails, when no monitored interface is left (the one given with
.Fl i ,
or the last one, has gone away; a supervisor can then restart it and
pick the interface up again if it comes back),
when waiting for packets fails, or when a report cannot be written or
mailed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr arpwatch 8 ,
.Xr tcpdump 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An neighbot contributors
